{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1339,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1915, Albert Einstein published a very\nimportant equation - no, not that one - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6600,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one he published didn’t just relate mass\nand energy, but mass, energy and gravity - this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12150,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation replaced the older “Newton’s\nlaw of Gravitation,” which you may be familiar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16340,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with, and it remains to this day our best\ndescription of how gravity works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20860,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like how F=ma is a mathematical description\nof how the acceleration of an object depends"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25730,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the forces applied to it, the Einstein\nEquation of general relativity relates the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion of mass and energy (the “T” on\nthe right) to the curvature of spacetime (the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34240,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“R’s” on the left)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36190,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Einstein didn’t just pull this equation\nout of thin air - it was the natural consequence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40160,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a long and careful consideration of key\nprinciples of physics combined with the advanced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44610,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematics of curved surfaces, and of course,\nagreement with the experimental observations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49270,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the day."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50270,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The equation, however, is deceptively simple."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53270,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This one single line is in fact an incredibly\nfancy shorthand for what’s actually a system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57840,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of ten second order partial differential equations\nrelating mass and energy to the curvature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61890,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spacetime, AND the the curvature R’s\nthemselves are a shorthand for more, um, complex,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expressions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66390,
    "dDurationMs": 2899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the point is this: after figuring out\nthat these equations matched up with Newton’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69289,
    "dDurationMs": 2746,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "law of gravitation for weak gravitational\nfields and speeds much slower than light speed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72035,
    "dDurationMs": 3124,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AND after showing that the equations correctly\npredicted a previously “unexplained-by-Newton’s-law”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75159,
    "dDurationMs": 3581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anomaly in the orbit of Mercury, Einstein\ntried to figure out what the equations had"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78740,
    "dDurationMs": 2230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to say about the universe as a whole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80970,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, all the matter and energy in the\nuniverse is too complicated to put into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85079,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations and have any hope of solving them,\nbut if you zoom out enough, you can approximate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89029,
    "dDurationMs": 4491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe as having a roughly constant\ndensity everywhere, and in every direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93520,
    "dDurationMs": 4199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Einstein was able to solve the equations\nfor a very simplified universe with constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97719,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density everywhere - the ten complicated equations\nreduced to just two simple ones: this one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102959,
    "dDurationMs": 4171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "says the curvature of space in the universe\nis proportional to the density, so more stuff"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107130,
    "dDurationMs": 4329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the universe means more curvature of space;\nand this one says that the density has to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111459,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112459,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which would mean there can’t be anything\nin the universe…"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115619,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Needless to say, this was a problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118179,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it turns out that there are two solutions\nto the problem - the one Einstein took, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123009,
    "dDurationMs": 1511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the one he didn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124520,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s solution was this: he knew (since\nhe had dived deep into the math) that it was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129441,
    "dDurationMs": 4518,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible to slightly change his equations;\nyou can add a single very simple term without"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133959,
    "dDurationMs": 2081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violating any key principles of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136040,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There wasn’t much other motivation for adding\nthis term, but it doesn’t change anything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139849,
    "dDurationMs": 3241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about how well the equations match up with\nNewton’s law when gravity is weak, or how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143090,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well they predict the orbit of Mercury, or\nwhatever , so maybe it was ok?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146750,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AND, crucially for Einstein, the new term\nchanges the equation for the density of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150700,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe: instead of saying “density equals\nzero,” it now says “density is proportional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155810,
    "dDurationMs": 1060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the new term”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156870,
    "dDurationMs": 3759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if the new term was non-zero, that meant\nthe universe could have stuff in it!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160629,
    "dDurationMs": 2111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Voila - solution number one - Einstein’s\nsolution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162740,
    "dDurationMs": 3609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other solution to how the universe can\nhave stuff in it was this: don’t assume"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166349,
    "dDurationMs": 3821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(as Einstein had) that the universe is static\nand unchanging."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170170,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The general understanding at the time was\nthat the universe didn’t expand or contract,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173730,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Einstein had also made a small but unfortunate\ntechnical error in his calculations which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177989,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appeared to prohibit the possibility of a\nchanging universe, so it’s not surprising"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181909,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Einstein didn’t see this solution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184249,
    "dDurationMs": 3231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it was there: if you don’t make the\nmathematical assumption that the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187480,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is static, and you don't make the technical\nerror Einstein did, you can find a different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191579,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "valid solution to Einstein’s equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193819,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which physicist Alexander Friedmann did."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196209,
    "dDurationMs": 2961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually he used the version of the equations\nwith the new term, knowing he could always"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199170,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "set that term to zero if it wasn’t real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201230,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the key part is he didn’t assume the\nuniverse was static."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204000,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Friedmann found that the ten equations again\nreduced to two: the first equation now describes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208260,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how the change in density of the universe\nrelates to its change in size: specifically,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212909,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it says that if the universe gets bigger,\nthen it gets less dense, which makes sense"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216689,
    "dDurationMs": 1421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- stuff’s literally spreading out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218110,
    "dDurationMs": 4749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second equation says that the deceleration\nof the universe is proportional to its density"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222859,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minus Einstein’s constant; that is, the\nstuff in the universe attracts itself gravitationally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228030,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the universe would have a tendency to pull\ninwards on itself, slowing any expansion and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232299,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possibly even contracting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234359,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless Einstein’s constant were real and\nhad a value big enough to balance or overpower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238329,
    "dDurationMs": 4401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravitational attraction . So that's the\nsolution Einstein didn't see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242730,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Later, once astronomers took sufficiently\ndetailed measurements, it turned out that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246450,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe WAS indeed expanding: distant\ngalaxies are moving away from us, and from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248040,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other - the universe is not static."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249040,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the measurements indicated that the universe\nwas expanding at a constant rate, at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250040,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within experimental error bars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251040,
    "dDurationMs": 1350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So Einstein’s equations didn’t appear\nto have any need for the extra term he had"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "added."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253390,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein was reported by physicist George\nGamow to have called it “his biggest blunder”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257560,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and while there’s no known documentation\nthat he ever actually said or wrote those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260410,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "words specifically, there’s plenty of record\nof him expressing disdain in other ways: “away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265259,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the cosmological term,” “I always\nhad a bad conscience,” “I found it very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269599,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ugly,” “such a constant appears…unjustified.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272199,
    "dDurationMs": 6301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, during Einstein’s lifetime, that was\ncertainly true - the term did appear unjustified."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278500,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, remember how Friedmann’s equations\npredicted that the universe should be attracting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282400,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself gravitationally and so the expansion\nshould be slowing down, unless Einstein’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286530,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant is real?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287940,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, in 1998 , decades after Einstein’s\ndeath, astronomers made the surprising discovery"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292910,
    "dDurationMs": 4789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the universe’s rate of expansion isn’t\nconstant, and it ISN’T slowing down - it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297699,
    "dDurationMs": 1211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting faster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298910,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so in a great, ironic twist, Einstein’s\nconstant does ultimately have a role in describing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303910,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe… though it turns out to be\na very different universe from what he had"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309180,
    "dDurationMs": 1519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "imagined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310699,
    "dDurationMs": 3821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you don’t want to make silly math mistakes\nlike Einstein, then you should probably head"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Brilliant.org, this video’s sponsor,\nto sharpen and hone your math and science"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "skills."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320320,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, Brilliant has a whole interactive\ncourse on cosmology and within it, a quiz"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324180,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specifically titled “The fate of the Universe”\nthat was tailor-made for giving you a deeper"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329650,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding than you can possibly gain from\nsimply watching a video like this one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332990,
    "dDurationMs": 4709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant also has fun daily challenges, which\nare bite-sized math and science-puzzles - like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337699,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this one about what happens to a thermometer\nif you put it in space, and then rotate it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342000,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does it still read the same temperature?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343860,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or hotter or colder?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345590,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant is offering 20% off of a premium\nsubscription to the first 200 MinutePhysics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349310,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "viewers to go to brilliant.org/minutephysics\n- that lets Brilliant know you came from here,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353750,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and gets you full access to all of Brilliant’s\ncourses, puzzles, and daily challenges."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357919,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, that’s brilliant.org/minutephysics\nso that you don’t mess up like Einstein."
    } ]
  } ]
}
